Practical 2: Create Your Own Wallet Using MetaMask
Aim: Create a wallet using MetaMask for crypto transactions.
Theory:
• Cryptocurrency is a digital currency that uses cryptography and runs on a blockchain, with no bank in the middle. Bitcoin (2009) was the first; others are Ethereum (2015), Litecoin and Ripple. All non-Bitcoin coins are called altcoins.
• Units are created by mining or bought from exchanges. You do not own a physical coin; you own a private key that lets you move a record on the ledger.
• Wallet types: hot wallet (online software, e.g. MetaMask, convenient but exposed to the internet) and cold wallet (offline hardware device, safer for large amounts).
• Each wallet has a public address (share it to receive funds) and a private key / 12-word seed phrase (never share it).
Steps: Install MetaMask, create a password, back up the seed phrase, confirm it, view the account address, switch to a test network, get test Ether from a faucet, and send a test transaction (same screens as Practical 1).
Conclusion: A MetaMask wallet was created and the concept of cryptocurrency transactions was understood.
Viva questions and answers
1. What is Bitcoin? The first and best-known cryptocurrency, created in 2009 by the pseudonymous Satoshi Nakamoto. It is a peer-to-peer digital currency recorded on a public blockchain and secured by Proof of Work mining, with a fixed supply of 21 million coins.
2. What are the four most common cryptocurrency scams?
• Fake investment / Ponzi schemes: promise guaranteed high returns and pay old investors with new investors' money.
• Phishing: fake websites, emails or wallet apps that steal your seed phrase or password.
• Rug pulls / fake tokens: developers launch a token, collect funds, then disappear.
• Impersonation and giveaway scams: fake celebrity or romance profiles ask you to send crypto to get more back.
3. How safe are money e-transfers? Generally safe when done through a bank or trusted app, because they use encryption, two-factor authentication and fraud monitoring. Risks: phishing, fake payment requests, wrong recipient (transfers are usually irreversible), weak passwords and public Wi-Fi. Stay safe by verifying the recipient, using 2FA, not clicking unknown links and checking statements.
4. What is cryptojacking and how does it work? Cryptojacking is secretly using someone else's computer, phone or server to mine cryptocurrency. It works through malware or a malicious script on a web page that runs mining code in the background. Signs: slow device, overheating, high CPU use and battery drain. Prevention: antivirus, ad/script blockers, updated software and avoiding unknown downloads.
